<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The events the world can have happen to it (docs/design/23-events-and-storyteller.md). Each
  names a worker in the simulation assembly and carries the gates a storyteller will read; today
  the only thing that fires one is the debug menu's Events tab, which ignores the gates by design.
  The description is that tab's tooltip.
-->
<Defs>

  <!--
    A stack of meals falls out of the sky on to whatever is under it: the reference's cargo pod,
    with the meals falling bare rather than in a pod (owner, 2026-09-20). The event is the reward.

    Ten to twenty is a little under to a full stack (Item_Meal stacks to 20), so a drop is one
    haul and never a pile that needs two trips. fallTicks 360 is six seconds at 60 ticks a second:
    it was 120 and the owner saw it land almost before it had been seen falling. The height it
    starts from is presentation's (FallArc.DropHeight). earliestDay 1 keeps the first day quiet
    and minRefireDays 2 keeps drops from bunching; neither is read until a storyteller exists, and
    both are invited tuning when one does.
  -->
  <IncidentDef>
    <defName>Incident_SupplyDrop</defName>
    <label>supply drop</label>
    <description>Drops a stack of meals from the sky somewhere on the board. Lands on the next tick, so unpause to see it</description>
    <bulletinKey>ui.bulletin.supplydrop</bulletinKey>
    <favourability>Good</favourability>
    <category>Misc</category>
    <worker>SupplyDrop</worker>
    <earliestDay>1</earliestDay>
    <minRefireDays>2</minRefireDays>
    <weight>100</weight>
    <minColonists>1</minColonists>
    <maxFires>0</maxFires>
    <item>Item_Meal</item>
    <stackMin>10</stackMin>
    <stackMax>20</stackMax>
    <fallTicks>360</fallTicks>
  </IncidentDef>

  <!--
    Scrap metal from the sky (design 32 §14): the supply drop's own worker with another cargo, so a
    storyteller that can fire one can fire the other. Fifteen to thirty is under a stack of fifty,
    one haul, and enough for a generator's parts or a run of line. The owner's second source of
    scrap metal beside the wreckage; every gate is the supply drop's and every one is tuning.
  -->
  <IncidentDef>
    <defName>Incident_ScrapDrop</defName>
    <label>scrap drop</label>
    <description>Drops a stack of scrap metal from the sky somewhere on the board. Lands on the next tick, so unpause to see it</description>
    <bulletinKey>ui.bulletin.scrapdrop</bulletinKey>
    <favourability>Good</favourability>
    <category>Misc</category>
    <worker>SupplyDrop</worker>
    <earliestDay>1</earliestDay>
    <minRefireDays>2</minRefireDays>
    <weight>60</weight>
    <minColonists>1</minColonists>
    <maxFires>0</maxFires>
    <item>Item_Salvage</item>
    <stackMin>15</stackMin>
    <stackMax>30</stackMax>
    <fallTicks>360</fallTicks>
  </IncidentDef>
  <!--
    Medical supplies from the sky (design 37 section 5): the supply drop's worker with a third
    cargo, and the colony's only source of supplies after the starting kit. Four to eight is under
    a stack of ten, so one haul (owner's numbers). Weight 40, rarer than either other drop, is
    INVENTED and read by nothing until a storyteller exists.
  -->
  <IncidentDef>
    <defName>Incident_MedicalDrop</defName>
    <label>medical drop</label>
    <description>Drops a few medical supplies from the sky somewhere on the board. Lands on the next tick, so unpause to see it</description>
    <bulletinKey>ui.bulletin.medicaldrop</bulletinKey>
    <favourability>Good</favourability>
    <category>Misc</category>
    <worker>SupplyDrop</worker>
    <earliestDay>1</earliestDay>
    <minRefireDays>2</minRefireDays>
    <weight>40</weight>
    <minColonists>1</minColonists>
    <maxFires>0</maxFires>
    <item>Item_MedicalSupplies</item>
    <stackMin>4</stackMin>
    <stackMax>8</stackMax>
    <fallTicks>360</fallTicks>
  </IncidentDef>

  <!--
    A bandit with nobody left to fight and nothing left to break carried a stack off the board
    (docs/design/33-combat.md section 17; owner, 2026-09-24: "thieve items ... creating a negative
    event"). Written down by the world when it happens and never fired, so its worker is Recorded,
    which the debug menu's Events tab leaves off its list; the bulletin carries the item and the
    amount. The gates are the defaults and read by nothing, since no storyteller picks it.
  -->
  <IncidentDef>
    <defName>Incident_Theft</defName>
    <label>theft</label>
    <description>A bandit carried a stack off the board. Recorded when it happens; nothing fires it</description>
    <bulletinKey>ui.bulletin.theft</bulletinKey>
    <favourability>Bad</favourability>
    <category>ThreatSmall</category>
    <worker>Recorded</worker>
  </IncidentDef>

  <!--
    The same bandit with nothing it could reach to take: it walks off the board empty-handed.
    Neutral, because nothing was lost; on the panel so a player who watched it go knows it has.
  -->
  <IncidentDef>
    <defName>Incident_BanditLeft</defName>
    <label>bandit left</label>
    <description>A bandit walked off the board with nothing. Recorded when it happens; nothing fires it</description>
    <bulletinKey>ui.bulletin.banditleft</bulletinKey>
    <favourability>Neutral</favourability>
    <category>ThreatSmall</category>
    <worker>Recorded</worker>
  </IncidentDef>

  <!--
    A colonist broke (design 51 §5c). Bad, because a colonist lost control; on the panel so the
    player can see which break and jump to where it began. Recorded when it happens; nothing fires it.
  -->
  <IncidentDef>
    <defName>Incident_MentalBreak</defName>
    <label>mental break</label>
    <description>A colonist had a mental break. Recorded when it happens; nothing fires it</description>
    <bulletinKey>ui.bulletin.mentalbreak</bulletinKey>
    <favourability>Bad</favourability>
    <category>Misc</category>
    <worker>Recorded</worker>
  </IncidentDef>

</Defs>
